# $title

This folder is a Pandion server for your documents. It answers questions over HTTP from the pipeline in `pipeline.yaml`, and you build it into a container image.

## What is in this folder

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `pipeline.yaml` | The pipeline: categories, routing, the index store, the models. Access control is off; the file ends with an example. |
| `serve.py` | The server. Run it with `uvicorn serve:app`. |
| `policy.py` | Who is asking. Read the next section before you deploy. |
| `Dockerfile` | Builds the image: locked install, models pulled, non-root, one worker. |
| `pyproject.toml` | The dependencies. Run `uv lock` once and commit `uv.lock`. |
| `.env.example` | Every secret the server reads, by name. Copy it to `.env` and fill it in. Never commit `.env`. |
| `evals/` | Your questions (`questions.json`) and, once you record it, `baseline.json`. |
| `tests/test_quality.py` | The quality gate, a few lines over `pandion eval`. |

$key_note

## Who is asking

`policy.py` has a `GatewayPolicy`. It expects your gateway to sign the user in and pass the user's id in the header named by `USER_ID_HEADER` (default `x-user-id`). A request without that header is refused with a 401, so keep this server on a private network that only the gateway can reach.

`roles(request)` returns no roles. Fill it in from your identity system. Until you do, and once you turn `access:` on in `pipeline.yaml`, nobody sees any document.

## First deploy

The order matters. The server answers `/ready` with `false` until the index exists.

```
uv lock
docker build -t my-rag .
docker run --rm --env-file .env -v rag-index:/app/index -v "$$PWD/docs:/app/docs:ro" my-rag pandion sync pipeline.yaml
docker run --env-file .env -v rag-index:/app/index -p 8000:8000 my-rag
```

$source_note

Run the same `pandion sync pipeline.yaml` on a schedule (cron, or a Kubernetes CronJob) with the same image to pick up changed documents.

## The quality gate

Record the baseline by running the built image, never at build time: a build would put keys and documents in the image layers. Mount `evals/` so the file lands in this folder:

```
chmod a+w evals
docker run --rm --env-file .env -v "$$PWD/evals:/app/evals" my-rag \
  pandion eval pipeline.yaml evals/questions.json --record-baseline evals/baseline.json
```

Commit `evals/baseline.json`. From then on, run the gate with the same image:

```
docker run --rm --env-file .env -v "$$PWD/evals:/app/evals" my-rag \
  pandion eval pipeline.yaml evals/questions.json --baseline evals/baseline.json
```

It first checks that every expected passage is still in its document, then that no question that was found before is now missed. `tests/test_quality.py` is the same gate for CI with the pipeline's models available. Questions carry a `category:<name>` tag, so a category that got worse fails the gate even when the total did not.

## Secrets

Names only are in `.env.example`. Put the values in `.env` for a local run, or in your platform's secret store. A key is never written to `pipeline.yaml`.

## Where data goes

| Data | Where it is kept |
|---|---|
| Your documents | The `source:` in `pipeline.yaml`. They are never copied into the image. |
| The index | The `index.store` in `pipeline.yaml`. $store_note |
| Conversations and memories | The database in `DATABASE_URL`, else a SQLite file in `index/`. |
| Questions sent to a model | The model provider named in `answer` and in any step that calls a model. |
| Models | `/models` in the image, pulled at build time. The server never downloads. |

## Security checklist

- Keep the server on a private network behind your gateway, with TLS ended at the gateway.
- Do not publish `/metrics`. Metrics are off until you turn them on in `serve.py`; give them their own port.
- Fill in `roles()` before you turn `access:` on.
- Keep `.env` out of git and out of the image (`.gitignore` and `.dockerignore` already do).
- Rebuild the image to update; the lockfile makes the build repeatable.

## Call it

```
curl -H "x-user-id: ann" -H "content-type: application/json" \
  -d '{"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"What is the notice period?"}]}' \
  http://localhost:8000/chat
```

Add `"stream": true` to get server-sent events: `chat_rewrite`, `sources`, `token`, `done` (and `error` before `done` after a failure). In a browser or Node:

```js
const res = await fetch("/chat", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "content-type": "application/json", "x-user-id": "ann" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ messages: [{ role: "user", content: "What is the notice period?" }], stream: true }),
});
const reader = res.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
for (;;) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  console.log(decoder.decode(value)); // lines "event: ..." and "data: {...}"
}
```

## The local defaults are for laptops

LanceDB on `./index` and SQLite conversations work on one machine only. To run more than one copy, keep the index in Qdrant (`index_store="qdrant"` when you generate this project), and the conversations in a SQL database (`DATABASE_URL`; install the database's driver in `pyproject.toml`).
